1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,113
You already know the method: job description, scope, ceiling, trial.

2
00:00:04,113 --> 00:00:07,876
You have used it four times, once per agent, and it held every time.

3
00:00:07,876 --> 00:00:09,979
The trap is not forgetting the method.

4
00:00:09,979 --> 00:00:14,517
It is deciding, quietly, that a small change you are about to make does not really

5
00:00:14,517 --> 00:00:15,623
count as needing it.

6
00:00:15,623 --> 00:00:17,505
Why is the sentence so persuasive?

7
00:00:17,505 --> 00:00:22,098
Because it prices the risk by the effort, and the effort really is small: one node,

8
00:00:22,098 --> 00:00:25,197
two minutes, a credential you already have lying around.

9
00:00:25,197 --> 00:00:28,462
Humans reason like that everywhere, and permissions do not.

10
00:00:28,462 --> 00:00:32,889
A two minute change can double what an agent can reach, and the doubling arrives

11
00:00:32,889 --> 00:00:36,652
with none of the ceremony that made the original build feel serious.

12
00:00:36,652 --> 00:00:39,751
No blank canvas, no new workflow, no sense of beginning.

13
00:00:39,751 --> 00:00:44,344
Just a plus button on something that already works, on an afternoon when the change

14
00:00:44,344 --> 00:00:45,617
would help immediately.

15
00:00:45,617 --> 00:00:50,210
That is precisely the moment this lecture is for, and you will recognise it when it

16
00:00:50,210 --> 00:00:53,309
comes, because it will feel like the opposite of a risk.

17
00:00:53,859 --> 00:00:57,798
An agent's trust was earned by the specific thing it was built to do.

18
00:00:57,798 --> 00:01:02,137
Add a capability, and you have quietly built a slightly different agent that

19
00:01:02,137 --> 00:01:05,734
inherits the trust of the old one without having earned it yet.

20
00:01:05,734 --> 00:01:09,330
Make it concrete with the addition this section keeps circling.

21
00:01:09,330 --> 00:01:13,840
The chaser you built writes drafts and reads one sheet: its whole risk story is

22
00:01:13,840 --> 00:01:15,610
about words waiting for review.

23
00:01:15,610 --> 00:01:20,235
Give it a mail-search tool and it has changed category, from an agent that writes

24
00:01:20,235 --> 00:01:25,030
into a folder you control to an agent that reads from an account full of things that

25
00:01:25,030 --> 00:01:26,457
are none of its business.

26
00:01:26,457 --> 00:01:31,139
Same canvas, same name, same trusted history in the execution log, and a genuinely

27
00:01:31,139 --> 00:01:32,338
different risk shape.

28
00:01:32,338 --> 00:01:36,220
Section 7.4 already told you a prompt edit resets part of the trust.

29
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:40,730
A capability edit resets more, because it does not change how the agent judges.

30
00:01:40,730 --> 00:01:42,785
It changes what the agent can touch.

31
00:01:43,335 --> 00:01:47,607
Scan the middle column and notice it is always generous, because the feeling comes

32
00:01:47,607 --> 00:01:49,483
from what the addition does for you.

33
00:01:49,483 --> 00:01:53,755
The right column is what it does to your risk, and the two have no relationship at

34
00:01:53,755 --> 00:01:53,963
all.

35
00:01:53,963 --> 00:01:58,288
That mismatch is the entire reason the worksheet in the next lecture exists: not to

36
00:01:58,288 --> 00:02:02,507
slow you down, but to make the right column visible while the middle one is doing

37
00:02:02,507 --> 00:02:03,133
the talking.

38
00:02:03,683 --> 00:02:05,796
Three steps, and none of them take long.

39
00:02:05,796 --> 00:02:10,076
That is the entire point: the method is not slow enough to justify skipping, even

40
00:02:10,076 --> 00:02:14,091
under deadline pressure, even for something that looks perfectly safe on the

41
00:02:14,091 --> 00:02:14,514
surface.

42
00:02:14,514 --> 00:02:15,835
Put honest times on them.

43
00:02:15,835 --> 00:02:20,220
Writing what the new piece can reach is five minutes, and it is the five minutes in

44
00:02:20,220 --> 00:02:24,130
which "the mailbox" becomes "the Renewals label", which is the whole game.

45
00:02:24,130 --> 00:02:28,463
Checking the credential's real permission level is two minutes on a consent screen

46
00:02:28,463 --> 00:02:30,524
you were going to click through anyway.

47
00:02:30,524 --> 00:02:34,592
And trialling the new piece specifically means a week of glancing at what the

48
00:02:34,592 --> 00:02:38,713
addition actually did on each run, what a search tool searched, what it found,

49
00:02:38,713 --> 00:02:42,940
whether that matches what you meant, without re-trialling the parts that already

50
00:02:42,940 --> 00:02:43,944
earned their trust.

51
00:02:43,944 --> 00:02:48,277
Perhaps fifteen minutes of writing and a week of glances, against an addition that

52
00:02:48,277 --> 00:02:50,073
changed what your agent can touch.

53
00:02:50,073 --> 00:02:53,719
Nobody's deadline is tight enough for that trade to go the other way.

54
00:02:54,269 --> 00:02:58,307
And when you read that story, resist the comfortable distance of hindsight.

55
00:02:58,307 --> 00:03:02,776
You will not watch it as a spectator, because you have already had the thought that

56
00:03:02,776 --> 00:03:06,060
starts it, probably during Section 6, possibly word for word.

57
00:03:06,060 --> 00:03:10,421
The value of the next two lectures is not learning that the thought is dangerous.

58
00:03:10,421 --> 00:03:14,836
It is having a fifteen minute habit standing between the thought and the click, so

59
00:03:14,836 --> 00:03:19,305
the thought can stay what it usually is: a good idea, one worksheet away from being

60
00:03:19,305 --> 00:03:19,897
a safe one.

61
00:03:19,897 --> 00:03:24,151
Next lecture turns everything in this section so far into one worksheet you run

62
00:03:24,151 --> 00:03:28,512
before adding anything new, ever, to any agent you build, in this course or after

63
00:03:28,512 --> 00:03:28,673
it.
